INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - WITNESS STAND - DAY

An old courtroom of dark oak and brass, high windows letting in a smear of grey light. The gallery is half full. A clock above the door ticks louder than anyone would like.

JUDGE ODETTE MARCHAND (60s, white hair in a tight bun, reading glasses on a chain) sits behind the bench, hands steepled. At the prosecution table, ATTORNEY RUPERT KLINE (40s, sharp suit, a habit of straightening his cuffs) rises with a folder. And on the witness stand, with her hands flat on her knees, sits NURSE GWENDOLYN ASARE (50s, grey cardigan, wedding band worn thin).

She stares straight ahead. He crosses slowly toward her.

KLINE
Ms. Asare, thank you for being here.

ASARE
I was subpoenaed.

KLINE
So you were.

He smiles a little. She doesn't. The judge removes her glasses, polishes them on her sleeve, and puts them back.

KLINE (CONT'D)
You were on shift the night of March the ninth.

ASARE
Yes.

KLINE
Alone on the ward?

ASARE
With two aides. One went home sick at midnight.

KLINE
And the patient in bed four, Mr. Fennimore. He was your responsibility.

ASARE
He was.

KLINE
You administered his medication at three a.m.

ASARE
At ten past three.

Kline pauses. He had not expected correction. He glances at the judge. She gives him nothing.

KLINE
Ten past three. Thank you for the precision.

ASARE
I always chart the time.

He walks back to his table, lifts a single sheet from the folder, and holds it between two fingers like something dead.

KLINE
This is the medication record. It lists the dose as fifty milligrams.

ASARE
It does.

KLINE
The pharmacy log says five.

ASARE
It says five.

KLINE
So which is right?

She is silent. The clock ticks. Judge Marchand leans forward very slightly. In the gallery, someone coughs.

KLINE (CONT'D)
Ms. Asare?

ASARE
Both.

KLINE
Both?

ASARE
The pharmacy sent five. I gave fifty.

A murmur sweeps the room. Kline sets the sheet down. He is no longer smiling.

KLINE
Why?

ASARE
Because Dr. Whitfield wrote fifty.

KLINE
And the pharmacy?

ASARE
Made a mistake.

KLINE
And you followed the doctor's order over the pharmacy's.

ASARE
I always do.

KLINE
Even when the numbers disagree?

ASARE
Mr. Kline, I have been a nurse for thirty-one years. When a doctor writes an order, I do not second-guess it.

KLINE
Even when it kills a man?

She flinches. Her hand rises, a fraction, then falls back to her knee.

JUDGE MARCHAND
Mr. Kline.

KLINE
Withdrawn, Your Honour.

JUDGE MARCHAND
Ms. Asare, would you like a recess?

ASARE
No, Your Honour.

She draws a long breath. Then she raises her eyes, finds the back of the courtroom, and speaks to someone nobody else can see.

ASARE (CONT'D)
I knew when I gave it. I felt the weight of the syringe. I knew.

The room has gone entirely quiet. Judge Marchand removes her glasses again and does not polish them this time.

KLINE
(gently)
Then why did you do it?

ASARE
Because if I questioned him, I would have been wrong. And if I was right, he would have been wrong. And no one wins that.

She stops. Her whole body seems to loosen. The tension of weeks drops out of her shoulders like a coat.

KLINE
Thank you.

ASARE
Is that all?

KLINE
That is all.

He nods to the judge and sits. Asare stays where she is for a moment, hands in her lap, and then slowly rises. Her legs are unsteady. She walks down from the stand, past the table, past the gallery, to the back door.

She pushes it open herself. Light spills in. She goes out into it.

Judge Marchand watches the empty doorway. She picks up her gavel, then sets it down without striking.

JUDGE MARCHAND
We will adjourn until two.
